\section{Enrichment Providers}
\label{sec:enrichment-providers}

Not all providers support DOI lookups or provide abstracts.
We define which providers to use for enrichment and in what order.

Unlike search (where we query multiple providers and merge the resulting paper
sets), enrichment starts from an existing paper record and fills in missing
fields.
To maximize coverage, we try multiple providers in sequence and keep going as
long as any requested fields are still missing.

We also skip providers that are not configured. For example, IEEE Xplore and Web
of Science require API keys; if they are not available, we move on to the next
provider.

\begin{description}
\item[Semantic Scholar] Excellent abstract coverage, free API with optional
  key for higher rate limits.
\item[arXiv] When a paper is an arXiv preprint, arXiv provides the abstract.
\item[OpenAlex] Very broad coverage (250M+ works), always free.
\item[IEEE Xplore] Strong coverage for IEEE publications.
  Requires an API key.
\item[Web of Science] High-quality bibliographic metadata.
  Requires an API key.
\item[Crossref] The abstract the publisher deposited, if any, read
  from the work record the currency check fetches anyway
  (\cref{sec:crossref-abstract}).
\item[Europe PMC] The abstract of a record its search finds under the
  DOI (\cref{europepmc-module}).
\item[Scopus] Abstracts for venues the others index poorly, but only
  where Elsevier serves its [[COMPLETE]] view, that is, on the
  subscribing institution's network or with its token
  (\cref{sec:scopus-doi-lookup}); elsewhere it declines after a single
  request.
  It comes last because its quota is weekly and metered: we spend it
  only on what the free services could not supply.
\end{description}

Crossref and Europe PMC are not search providers but per-DOI lookups,
and they fill the abstract only.
They come after the other search providers, so that the abstract of a
provider that also fills the other fields wins whenever there is one,
and before Scopus, whose weekly quota they save.
Crossref comes before Europe PMC: [[scholar enrich]] checks every
paper's currency against Crossref anyway, and the abstract comes with
the same cached response, so asking Crossref adds no request to the run
(apart from one, once, for a record cached before abstracts were kept),
while Europe PMC is a request of its own.

<<constants>>=
# Providers to try for DOI-based metadata enrichment (in priority order)
ENRICHMENT_PROVIDERS = [
    "s2", "arxiv", "openalex", "ieee", "wos",
    "crossref", "europepmc", "scopus",
]

# Global cache for enriched paper data (refs/cites)
# Stored in data dir (not cache dir) since fetching refs/cites is expensive
ENRICHMENT_CACHE_DIR = "enrichment_cache"
@

The loop below asks every name in the list the same question,
[[get_paper_by_doi]], and merges the answer.
Crossref's and Europe PMC's clients answer it too
(\cref{sec:crossref-abstract,europepmc-module}), but they are not in
the provider registry: they are not search providers, and registering
them would put them in [[scholar providers list]], [[providers check]]
and every default search.
So [[enrichment_source]] resolves a name: the shared client of an
abstract service, else the registered provider.
The services' modules are imported here; [[scholar.crossref]] is
loaded by the preprint fallback already.

<<constants>>=
# Per-DOI services that fill fields the search providers left empty,
# with the fields they fill. Not search providers: not registered.
ABSTRACT_SOURCE_FIELDS = {
    "crossref": ("abstract",),
    "europepmc": ("abstract",),
}
<<enrichment functions>>=
def abstract_source(name: str):
    """Return the shared client of the abstract service `name`."""
    module = {"crossref": crossref, "europepmc": europepmc}[name]
    return module.enrichment_source()


def enrichment_source(name: str):
    """Return what to ask for DOI lookups under `name`, or None.

    An abstract service's client (`ABSTRACT_SOURCE_FIELDS`), else the
    registered provider of that name.
    """
    if name in ABSTRACT_SOURCE_FIELDS:
        return abstract_source(name)
    return get_provider(name)
@

A user naming the sources ([[--enrich-providers]]) should hear about
a misspelt name rather than get a run that silently asked nothing under
it.
The names that can answer are the registered providers that have a DOI
lookup, which includes the preprint servers' OpenAlex lookups, and the
two abstract services.

<<enrichment functions>>=
def enrichment_source_names() -> list[str]:
    """Return the names `enrich_paper` can ask, sorted."""
    from scholar.providers import PROVIDERS

    names = {
        name for name, provider in PROVIDERS.items()
        if hasattr(provider, "get_paper_by_doi")
    }
    return sorted(names | set(ABSTRACT_SOURCE_FIELDS))
@


\section{Text that is not an abstract}
\label{sec:doubtful-abstracts}

A service's abstract field does not always hold an abstract.
A screen that ran [[scholar enrich]] found, by reading, that some of
the abstracts filled from OpenAlex were a conflict-of-interest
statement, a data-availability statement, a note about supplementary
material or a fragment of a table
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/287}).
The text is the service's, not a parsing error of ours, but it used to
be stored as the abstract without a mark, and from then on the record
counted as having one: no further source was asked
([[needs_enrichment]]), and a screener or the classifier judged the
paper on a disclosure statement as if it were its abstract.

We cannot tell an abstract from other text in general, and a filter
that drops text would drop real abstracts too.
So the check only \emph{flags}: [[doubtful_abstract]] returns a short
reason when the text looks like something other than an abstract, and
[[None]] otherwise.
Nothing is ever deleted because of it.
What a flag changes is said where it is used: [[scholar enrich]] asks
further sources and lets a real abstract from one of them replace the
doubtful text, keeping the doubtful one when none is found
(\cref{sec:replace-doubtful-abstract}); [[sessions show]] and the report
mark and count the doubtful abstracts.

\subsection{What the check looks for}

The kinds come from the report of the screen: a conflict-of-interest
or disclosure statement; a data-availability, funding or
acknowledgement statement; a keywords line; an author-address or
correspondence block; a note about supplementary material; a fragment
of a table or of a reference list; and text too short to be an
abstract.
Every pattern below, and the length floor, is \emph{scholar's own
choice}: we know of no rule for what an abstract is that we could cite,
and none of the numbers is an established threshold.
They are deliberately few and conservative, so that a doubtful flag is
rare and explainable, and they were calibrated on real abstracts
(\cref{sec:check-abstracts-command,sec:abstract-check-calibration})
and adjusted.
The check cannot be complete.
A statement written freely, not in one of the shapes below, slips
through, and an article's opening paragraph stored as its abstract (for
article types that have none) cannot be told from an abstract at all.
The mark points at candidates; a reader decides.

The one design rule is that a pattern only matches at the \emph{start}
of the text.
A real abstract may well say, in its last sentence, \enquote{Funding:
none} or \enquote{The authors declare no competing interests}; a
statement that merely \emph{is} one of those things begins with it.
And a real abstract can begin with the same words used as a topic:
\enquote{Disclosure of HIV status to children \dots},
\enquote{Funding agencies increasingly \dots}, \enquote{Correspondence
analysis is \dots}, \enquote{Keyword search over databases \dots}.
So a word that can be a topic counts as a heading only when what
follows it shows that it is one:
\begin{description}
\item[A delimiter] a colon, a full stop, an en or em dash, or a hyphen
  between spaces (\enquote{Funding: \dots}, \enquote{Keywords --
  \dots}), unless a lower-case indefinite article follows it:
  \enquote{Funding: a key determinant of research output \dots} and
  \enquote{E-mail: a medium for feedback \dots} are definitions that
  begin real abstracts, found in review; a statement under a heading
  begins otherwise (\enquote{Funding: This research \dots},
  \enquote{Funding: A grant from \dots}).
  A hyphen directly after the word is not one: \enquote{Email-based
  phishing} and \enquote{Acknowledgement-based protocols} are topics.
\item[The end of the text] a heading alone.
\item[A run-in sentence] the heading followed directly by a capitalised
  word from a short list (\enquote{Conflict of interest The authors
  declare \dots}), which is how a heading reads once the service has
  flattened its markup; in prose the next word is lower case
  (\enquote{Conflicts of interest are common}).
  The list is matched case-sensitively, the rest of the pattern not.
\end{description}
A few whole openings are specific enough to need no such evidence:
\enquote{The authors declare no competing interests},
\enquote{This work was supported by}, \enquote{The data that support
the findings}, \enquote{Corresponding author}.

[[heading]] builds the pattern of a heading from its words and,
optionally, the run-in words it accepts.

<<abstract check pattern helpers>>=
def heading(words: str, run_in: str = "") -> str:
    """Return a pattern for the heading `words` used as a heading.

    It matches when a delimiter or the end of the text follows, or one
    of the capitalised `run_in` words (matched case-sensitively).
    """
    after = HEADING_END + (rf"|\s+(?-i:{run_in})(?!\w)" if run_in else "")
    return rf"(?:{words})(?:{after})"


HEADING_END = (
    r"\s*[:.\u2013\u2014](?!\s*(?-i:an?)\s)|\s+-\s(?!\s*(?-i:an?)\s)"
    r"|\s*$"
)
DISCLOSING_PERSON = (
    r"(?:(?-i:Drs?|Prof|Professor)\.?\s+(?:(?-i:[A-Z])[\w'.-]*\s+){1,3}"
    r"|the authors?\s+)"
)
DISCLOSURE_PHRASE = (
    r"(?:(?:has|have) received (?:\w+ ){0,3}?(?:honoraria"
    r"|(?:consulting|speaker(?:'s)?|personal) fees|grants?"
    r"|research support)"
    r"|serves? as (?:an? )?(?:\w+ ){0,3}?editor"
    r"|(?:is|are) (?:an )?employees? of"
    r"|reports? (?:grants|personal fees|consulting fees))"
)
@

Each kind gets its openings, and the reason [[doubtful_abstract]]
returns is the kind's name, so that counts by reason are counts by
kind.

\begin{description}
\item[Conflict-of-interest statement] the headings
  \enquote{Conflict(s) of interest}, \enquote{Competing interests},
  \enquote{Declaration of (competing) interest(s)} and
  \enquote{(Financial) disclosure(s)}, each optionally followed by
  \enquote{statement}; and the statements that open without a heading:
  \enquote{The authors declare no \dots}, \enquote{The author has no
  \dots} (only followed by competing, conflict or financial words) and
  \enquote{No (potential) conflict of interest was reported}.
  \enquote{The authors report no \dots} is left out on purpose: a case
  report may begin \enquote{The authors report no difference \dots}.
  A disclosure can also be prose about one person or the authors, with
  no heading: \enquote{Dr.~X serves as an assistant editor for \dots},
  \enquote{The author has received honoraria \dots}.
  Such an opening counts when a title (Dr, Prof, Professor) and up to
  three capitalised names, or \enquote{the author(s)}, are followed by
  one of a few disclosure phrases: has (have) received honoraria,
  consulting, speaker or personal fees, grants or research support;
  serves as an editor; is an employee of; reports grants or fees.
\item[Data-availability statement] the headings \enquote{Data
  availability}, \enquote{Availability of data (and materials)} and
  \enquote{Data sharing}, optionally with \enquote{statement}; and
  \enquote{The data that support the findings}, the usual opening of
  such a statement.
\item[Funding statement] the headings \enquote{Funding} and
  \enquote{Financial support} (optionally with statement, information
  or source); \enquote{This work (study, research, project) was
  supported (funded) by}; and \enquote{The author(s) received no
  (specific) funding}.
\item[Acknowledgements] the heading in both spellings.
\item[Opens with thanks] \enquote{We (would like to) thank},
  \enquote{The authors are grateful}: an acknowledgement without its
  heading, or a letter in reply (\enquote{We would like to thank
  Dr.~X for the comment on our editorial \dots}), which the
  calibration found stored as abstracts
  (\cref{sec:abstract-check-calibration}).
\item[Keywords line] \enquote{Keywords}, \enquote{Key words} and
  \enquote{Index terms} as headings, and \enquote{KEYWORDS} in capitals
  however it continues (a capitalised heading is not a topic).
\item[Author address or correspondence] the headings
  \enquote{Correspondence}, \enquote{Address for correspondence},
  \enquote{E-mail (address)}, \enquote{Corresponding author}; and
  \enquote{Correspondence to} followed by a colon or a capitalised name,
  and \enquote{Correspondence should be addressed}.
  \enquote{Corresponding authors often differ \dots} and
  \enquote{Correspondence to reality is \dots} are topics, found in
  review: a bare \enquote{Corresponding author} without a delimiter
  no longer counts.
\item[Supplementary-material note] \enquote{Supplementary (supplemental)
  material, information, data or files} as a heading or followed within
  the sentence by \enquote{is (are) available, provided, included};
  \enquote{Supporting information} as a heading; \enquote{The online
  version \dots{} contains supplementary material}; and
  \enquote{Additional supporting information}.
  \enquote{Supplementary feeding of preschool children} is a topic and
  does not match.
\item[Table fragment] \enquote{Table} or \enquote{TABLE} with a number,
  arabic or roman: a table's caption, where the abstract should begin.
  \enquote{Table tennis} does not match, and neither does a label
  followed by a verb (\enquote{Table 1 shows \dots}, \enquote{TABLE II
  summarises \dots}), which is a sentence about a table.
\item[Reference-list fragment] \enquote{References} or
  \enquote{Bibliography} as a heading or followed by a first entry
  (\enquote{1.}, \enquote{[1]}), and a text that opens with
  \enquote{[1]} \emph{and} has \enquote{[2]} within
  [[REFERENCE_ENTRY_CHARS]] characters.
  The second entry is what tells a list from numbered paragraphs, as
  some journals print their articles (\enquote{[1] The mission was
  \dots}), which the calibration found stored as abstracts of many
  thousand characters.
  A reference entry is short; a numbered paragraph is not.
\end{description}

