The [[REFERENCE_ENTRY_CHARS]] are our own choice too: two hundred
characters hold an ordinary entry (authors, title, venue, year) and are
far shorter than a paragraph of running text.

<<abstract check pattern helpers>>=
REFERENCE_ENTRY_CHARS = 200
STUDY_VERBS = (
    r"examines|investigates|explores|studies|presents|reports|describes"
    r"|aims|analy[sz]es|proposes|develops|evaluates|compares|discusses"
    r"|introduces|focuses|shows"
)
@

A funding clause can also open a real abstract: \enquote{This research
is funded by X and examines Y} goes on to say what the research does.
So \enquote{This work was supported by} counts only when the sentence
does not continue, within two hundred characters, with \enquote{and}
and one of a few verbs that report a study ([[STUDY_VERBS]]).

A run-in word must end where the text's word ends (\enquote{The}, not
\enquote{Theory}), which [[heading]] says as \enquote{not followed by
a letter or digit} rather than as a word boundary: the reference
heading's run-in is an entry number such as \enquote{1.}, after which
comes a space, and there is no word boundary between a full stop and a
space.

All openings go into one pattern, one named group per kind, so that a
text is matched once and the group that matched names the kind.

<<abstract check constants>>=
DOUBTFUL_PATTERN = re.compile(
    "|".join(
        f"(?P<kind{index}>{'|'.join(openings)})"
        for index, (_, openings) in enumerate(DOUBTFUL_OPENINGS)
    ),
    re.IGNORECASE,
)
@

The start of the text is found after what can come before a heading
without being part of it: white space, inline tags a service left
([[<p>]], \cref{sec:clean-provider-text}), footnote marks and quotes
(\enquote{*Corresponding author}), and the word \enquote{Abstract}
that some services put first.
An opening bracket is kept, since \enquote{[1]} is a pattern of its
own.
A real abstract that begins \enquote{Abstract interpretation is \dots}
loses its first word only for the matching, and its next words match
no opening.

