\subsection{A book's table of contents}

The calibration also found tables of contents of books stored as their
abstracts, thousands of characters long, that open with front-matter
headings: \enquote{Acknowledgments. Preface. How to Read This Book.
Chapter 1 \dots}.
They opened with the acknowledgements heading, but what they are is a
table of contents, so they get a reason of their own, and the check for
it comes before the openings above.
A text counts as one when it opens with a front-matter heading
(acknowledgements, preface, foreword, contents or table of contents,
a list of figures, tables or abbreviations) and a first chapter or
part (\enquote{Chapter 1}, \enquote{Part I}, \enquote{1.}~followed
by a capitalised word) comes within [[FRONT_MATTER_CHARS]] characters.
A real abstract may begin with the word \enquote{Contents} or
\enquote{Preface}, but then as the start of a sentence, followed by a
lower-case word (\enquote{Preface to the special issue \dots},
\enquote{Contents of school lunches \dots}, found in review), so a
front-matter word counts only when no lower-case word follows it.
One lower-case word is allowed: \enquote{by} followed by a capitalised
name, since a book's foreword is often listed with its author
(\enquote{Foreword by Ada Example. Author's Note. Chapter One \dots}),
and the rule above dropped three true tables of contents of that shape
in the third calibration run (\cref{sec:abstract-check-calibration}).
\enquote{Foreword by the editors of this volume argues \dots} is still
a sentence: \enquote{by} is followed by a lower-case word.

<<abstract check constants>>=
TABLE_OF_CONTENTS = "table of contents"
FRONT_MATTER_CHARS = 300
FRONT_MATTER = re.compile(
    r"(?:acknowledge?ments?|preface|foreword|(?:table of )?contents"
    r"|list of (?:figures|tables|abbreviations))\b"
    r"(?!\s+(?!by\s+(?-i:[A-Z]))(?-i:[a-z]))",
    re.IGNORECASE,
)
FIRST_CHAPTER = re.compile(
    r"\b(?:chapter|part) (?:1|one|i)\b|(?:^|\s)1\.\s+(?-i:[A-Z])",
    re.IGNORECASE,
)
<<abstract check functions>>=
def is_table_of_contents(head: str, start: int) -> bool:
    """Whether `head` opens, at `start`, like a book's table of contents."""
    if not FRONT_MATTER.match(head, start):
        return False
    window = head[start:start + FRONT_MATTER_CHARS]
    return FIRST_CHAPTER.search(window) is not None
@

\subsection{Statements in the first sentence}

Two statements the report of the screen named do not begin with a
fixed word: a note about supplementary material that names what is
available first (\enquote{Prompts, code and evaluation details
(Supplementary Texts 1--10) are available through \dots}), and a
data-availability statement written the same way.
For them the check reads the first sentence, up to the first full stop,
question or exclamation mark followed by a space, but not further than
[[FIRST_SENTENCE_CHARS]] characters, and only when the sentence is
\emph{about} availability: its first \enquote{is (are) available}
comes within [[AVAILABLE_WITHIN]] characters of its start, with no
semicolon before it.
What is said to be available must then be named before
\enquote{available}:
\begin{description}
\item[Supplementary-material note] supplementary (supplemental)
  material, information, data, files, tables, figures, appendices or
  texts, or supporting information ([[SUPPLEMENTARY_MATERIAL]]).
  The word \enquote{supplementary} alone is not enough:
  \enquote{Supplemental Instruction is available at many universities}
  and \enquote{\dots available on request for supplementary-insured
  patients} are topics, found in review.
\item[Data-availability statement] data, datasets, code, materials,
  transcripts, scripts, software, questionnaires or protocols
  ([[AVAILABLE_THINGS]]), said to be \enquote{available (up)on
  (reasonable) request}; \enquote{Genetic testing is available on
  request} is a topic.
\end{description}
The condition on where \enquote{available} comes is what keeps a real
one-sentence abstract that ends \enquote{\dots; the data that support
the findings are available on request} unflagged.
A first sentence that says that models \enquote{are available through
open repositories} names neither, and is not flagged; for that reason a
repository alone is not taken as a sign, though availability statements
often name one.
The [[AVAILABLE_WITHIN]] characters are our own choice: enough for a
subject that lists a few things (prompts, code, data), too few for a
sentence that reports a study first.

<<abstract check constants>>=
FIRST_SENTENCE_CHARS = 300
AVAILABLE_WITHIN = 120
FIRST_SENTENCE = re.compile(r".*?(?:[.!?](?=\s)|$)")
SUPPLEMENTARY_MATERIAL = re.compile(
    r"\bsupplement(?:ary|al) (?:materials?|information|data|files?"
    r"|tables?|figures?|appendi(?:x|ces)|texts?)\b|\bsupporting information\b",
    re.IGNORECASE,
)
AVAILABLE_THINGS = re.compile(
    r"\b(?:data|datasets?|code|materials?|transcripts?|scripts?|software"
    r"|questionnaires?|protocols?)\b",
    re.IGNORECASE,
)
IS_AVAILABLE = re.compile(r"\b(?:is|are) (?:\w+ )?available\b", re.IGNORECASE)
ON_REQUEST = re.compile(
    r"\bavailable (?:up)?on (?:\w+ )?request\b", re.IGNORECASE
)
<<abstract check functions>>=
def first_sentence_statement(head: str, start: int) -> tuple[str, int] | None:
    """The kind of statement `head`'s first sentence is, and its end.

    None unless the first sentence (from `start`, at most
    `FIRST_SENTENCE_CHARS` long) says early that something is available
    (`AVAILABLE_WITHIN`, no semicolon before) and names, before that,
    supplementary material, or data or code said to be available on
    request.
    """
    window = head[start:start + FIRST_SENTENCE_CHARS]
    sentence = FIRST_SENTENCE.match(window).group()
    end = start + len(sentence)
    available = IS_AVAILABLE.search(sentence)
    if (available is None or available.start() > AVAILABLE_WITHIN
            or ";" in sentence[:available.start()]):
        return None
    subject = sentence[:available.start()]
    if SUPPLEMENTARY_MATERIAL.search(subject):
        return "supplementary-material note", end
    if (AVAILABLE_THINGS.search(subject)
            and ON_REQUEST.search(sentence, available.start())):
        return "data-availability statement", end
    return None
@

\subsection{A statement followed by the abstract}

An opening statement does not always \emph{replace} the abstract;
sometimes it only precedes it.
The calibration found a conference abstract that began with its
authors' disclosures (\enquote{Disclosure: [initials]: None. \dots})
and went on with \enquote{Background:} and the structured abstract, and
a record whose keywords line was followed by \enquote{Abstract.} and
the abstract.
Both hold a real abstract, and flagging them was wrong.
So a text whose opening statement is followed, anywhere later, by an
abstract's own heading is not doubtful, provided what follows that
heading passes the length floor by itself.
The headings are \enquote{Abstract} and the labels of a structured
abstract: Background, Objective(s), Purpose, Introduction, Aim(s),
Methods, capitalised or in capitals, at the start of a sentence or
after punctuation, and followed by a colon or a full stop
([[ABSTRACT_HEADING]]).
The rule applies to the statements (disclosures, availability, funding,
acknowledgements, thanks, keywords, addresses, supplementary notes),
not to a table of contents, whose chapter list holds an
\enquote{Introduction.} as a matter of course, nor to the fragments,
which are not statements.

<<abstract check constants>>=
ABSTRACT_HEADING = re.compile(
    r"(?:^|(?<=[\s.;:]))(?:Abstract|ABSTRACT|Background|BACKGROUND"
    r"|Objectives?|OBJECTIVES?|Purpose|PURPOSE|Introduction|INTRODUCTION"
    r"|Aims?|AIMS?|Methods|METHODS)\s*[:.]"
)
NOT_RESCUED = {"table fragment", "reference-list fragment"}
<<abstract check functions>>=
def abstract_follows(text: str, end: int) -> bool:
    """Whether an abstract's heading after `end` leads a long enough text.

    `text` has its white space collapsed; the text after the heading
    must reach `MIN_ABSTRACT_CHARS`.
    """
    for heading_match in ABSTRACT_HEADING.finditer(text, end):
        if len(text) - heading_match.end() >= MIN_ABSTRACT_CHARS:
            return True
    return False
@

\subsection{Text too short to be an abstract}

A text of a few words is not an abstract whatever it says: a title
repeated, \enquote{No abstract available}, a single sentence of an
editorial.
We flag text shorter than [[MIN_ABSTRACT_CHARS]] characters, after
white space is collapsed.
The hundred characters are our own choice,
set low so that a short but real abstract is not
flagged, and the floor is one of the numbers the calibration is for.
A pattern's reason wins over the length's: \enquote{Conflict of
interest: none.} is short, but its kind says more.

<<abstract check constants>>=
MIN_ABSTRACT_CHARS = 100
TOO_SHORT = f"fewer than {MIN_ABSTRACT_CHARS} characters"
@

The floor counts characters, and a character of Chinese or Japanese
holds more of a text than a letter of a Latin script: in review a
complete Chinese abstract of 73 characters and a Japanese one of 58
were flagged as too short.
So a character that Unicode classes as East Asian wide or fullwidth
([[unicodedata.east_asian_width]] [[W]] or [[F]]) counts
[[WIDE_CHAR_WEIGHT]] times.
The weight is our own choice: with 2 those two abstracts weigh 146 and
116 and pass, a Chinese text of 48 characters still does not, and a
service's short notice such as a four-character \enquote{peer
reviewed} stays far below the floor.
The calibration (\cref{sec:abstract-check-calibration}) is what can
show whether it is right.
The characters are weighed only for a text whose head is short already,
so the cost is paid by few texts.

<<abstract check constants>>=
WIDE_CHAR_WEIGHT = 2
<<abstract check functions>>=
def weighed_length(text: str) -> int:
    """The length of `text`, East Asian wide characters weighed.

    A character of `east_asian_width` W or F counts `WIDE_CHAR_WEIGHT`
    times, any other once.
    """
    wide = sum(
        1 for char in text if unicodedata.east_asian_width(char) in "WF"
    )
    return len(text) + wide * (WIDE_CHAR_WEIGHT - 1)
@

\subsection{The check}

[[doubtful_abstract]] judges text alone; it does not know who gave
it.
An abstract given by hand ([[sessions set-abstract]], in the review
module) is never judged: the screener read it and chose it, so its
callers skip it (\cref{sec:replace-doubtful-abstract}).
No text, or only white space, is not doubtful but missing, which
[[needs_enrichment]] already treats as such.
A list of paragraphs, as older Web of Science records stored the
abstract (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/290},
\cref{sec:abstract-paragraphs}), is judged as the text its paragraphs
make ([[join_paragraphs]]); any other value that is not text is not
judged, since a check that reads stored data must not fail on it.

The order is: a table of contents first, then the openings, then the
first sentence; a statement found by either of the last two is let off
when an abstract follows it; and the length floor last.

<<abstract check functions>>=
def doubtful_abstract(text) -> str | None:
    """Return why `text` looks like something other than an abstract.

    The reason names the kind (a table of contents, `DOUBTFUL_OPENINGS`
    matched at the start of the text, a statement in its first
    sentence) or says the text is shorter than `MIN_ABSTRACT_CHARS`. A
    statement followed by an abstract's heading and a long enough text
    is not doubtful. None when the text is not doubtful, and for a
    missing or blank text or a value that is not text (a list of
    paragraphs is judged joined). The text is never changed.
    """
    if isinstance(text, (list, tuple)):
        text = join_paragraphs(text)
    if not isinstance(text, str) or not text:
        return None
    head = " ".join(text[:HEAD_CHARS].split()) or " ".join(text.split())
    if not head:
        return None
    start = TEXT_START.match(head).end()
    if is_table_of_contents(head, start):
        return TABLE_OF_CONTENTS
    statement = opening_statement(head, start)
    if statement is not None:
        reason, end = statement
        if reason in NOT_RESCUED or not abstract_follows(
            " ".join(text.split()), end
        ):
            return reason
        return None
    if (len(head) < MIN_ABSTRACT_CHARS
            and weighed_length(" ".join(text.split())) < MIN_ABSTRACT_CHARS):
        return TOO_SHORT
    return None


def opening_statement(head: str, start: int) -> tuple[str, int] | None:
    """The kind of the opening at `start` of `head`, and where it ends.

    The openings of `DOUBTFUL_OPENINGS` first, then the first sentence
    (`first_sentence_statement`); None for neither.
    """
    match = DOUBTFUL_PATTERN.match(head, start)
    if match is not None:
        index = int(match.lastgroup.removeprefix("kind"))
        return DOUBTFUL_OPENINGS[index][0], match.end()
    return first_sentence_statement(head, start)
@

Only the head of the text is read, because collapsing the white space
of a whole abstract is what the check would otherwise spend its time on
(about four times as long on abstracts of a thousand characters, in our
measurement while writing this).
[[HEAD_CHARS]] is far longer than any opening needs, whose longest
spans are the 80 and 60 characters allowed within a supplementary-material
sentence.
The head's length also settles the floor: collapsing white space never
makes a text longer, so a head that is not too short belongs to a text
that is not, and the whole text is collapsed only when the head is
short.

<<abstract check constants>>=
HEAD_CHARS = 500
@

The check is a module of its own, [[scholar.abstract_check]], that
imports nothing but the standard library and [[scholar.utils]]:
[[sessions show]] and the report run it over every abstract of a
session, and importing this enrichment module would load the provider
clients and the Crossref cache for nothing.

