\subsection{What the calibration showed}
\label{sec:abstract-check-calibration}

The command is run over copies of the developer's review sessions after
each change to the rules.
Its counts are scholar's own measurements on one developer's sessions,
not rates to expect elsewhere; they are given here so that the next
change can be compared with them, and the texts are described by their
shape only.

\paragraph{First run, 2026-11-20.}
Of 147{,}108 distinct stored abstracts the first version of the check
flagged 667, or 0.45~per cent.
The length floor accounted for nearly all of it, 649 texts: mostly
what a service puts in the field when it has no abstract (a note that
the item is peer reviewed, that a thesis was catalogued from its PDF,
that access must be requested from a librarian, the name of a thesis
series, a dedication), and some real one-line descriptions, which the
mark shows a reader as what they are.
The floor stayed.
The openings flagged the rest: 11 under acknowledgements, which were
letters in reply that open with thanks and books' tables of contents
(hence the two reasons of their own); two conflict-of-interest
statements and two keywords lines, one of each true and one a real
abstract after the statement; two reference-list fragments, both long
articles printed as numbered paragraphs; and one funding footnote,
true.
The false positives led to the rule that an abstract after the
statement lets it off (\cref{sec:doubtful-abstracts}) and to the
second entry a reference list needs.
The run also met 77 abstracts stored as lists of paragraphs, on which
the first version failed (\cref{sec:abstract-paragraphs}).
The disclosures about a person and the statements found in the first
sentence were added afterwards, from the cases the report of the screen
gave that the first version missed.

\paragraph{Second run, 2026-11-20.}
With those rules, 145{,}772 abstracts were judged (distinct papers;
36{,}981 papers without an abstract were not), and 729 were doubtful,
0.50~per cent: 648 under the floor, 68 tables of contents, 6 opening
with thanks, 2 conflict-of-interest statements, 2 supplementary-material
notes, 1 keywords line, 1 funding statement and 1 acknowledgements
heading; 22 stored abstracts were lists.
A review of the rules then found real abstracts they would flag that
the sessions happened not to hold: topics that open like the
first-sentence statements (\enquote{Supplemental Instruction is
available \dots}), a table label followed by a verb, a front-matter
word that starts a sentence, a heading followed by an indefinite
article, a funding clause that goes on to say what the research does,
and short but complete abstracts in Chinese and Japanese.
Each is a test now, and the rules were narrowed for it (the
statements in the first sentence, the delimiter of a heading, the
table, front-matter and funding openings, and the weighed floor).

\paragraph{Third run, 2026-11-20.}
On the narrowed rules, 145{,}772 abstracts were judged and 724 were
doubtful, 0.50~per cent: 647 under the floor, 65 tables of contents, 6
opening with thanks, 2 conflict-of-interest statements, 1
supplementary-material note, 1 keywords line, 1 funding statement and
1 acknowledgements heading; 22 stored abstracts were lists.
Against the second run five flags dropped and none was added: a
complete Chinese abstract (the weighed floor, as intended); a text
whose publisher's sentence about supplemental digital content was
followed by the real abstract (rightly not flagged); and three true
tables of contents that open with a foreword and its author's name,
lost to the rule that a front-matter word followed by a lower-case word
starts a sentence.
After this run, a front-matter word followed by \enquote{by} and a
capitalised name counts as a heading again
(\cref{sec:doubtful-abstracts}).

Let's verify the command on two invented session files that share a
paper, with a hand-given abstract and a paper without one, and that
the files are left exactly as they were.

