\section{Single Paper Enrichment}

The core enrichment function takes a paper and returns an enriched version
with any missing fields filled in from other providers.
If the paper already has all fields or lacks a DOI (making lookup impossible),
we return the original paper unchanged.

The function is general-purpose: by default it enriches all common fields
(title, authors, abstract, year, venue, pdf\_url), but callers can specify a
subset of fields if they only care about specific ones.

Some metadata is optional and not always available.
For example, author keywords/index terms may be missing in many databases.
To avoid triggering enrichment for every paper, these fields are typically
requested explicitly by passing them in the [[fields]] parameter.

Callers can also override which providers to try (and in what order) by passing
provider names in [[providers]].
A caller that needs to know why a field stayed empty---an answer
without it, or a failed lookup---passes a [[LookupOutcome]] in
[[outcome]] (\cref{sec:enrich-lookup-outcomes}).

After the providers have had their turn, and whenever [[pdf_url]] is among
the requested fields, we ask Unpaywall (\cref{unpaywall-module}) for the
paper's open-access status.  Unpaywall is not a provider: it answers a
different question (\enquote{is there a free copy, and where?}) and answers
it for DOIs the providers know nothing about, so it runs regardless of whether
the provider loop found anything.  It fills [[oa_status]] and [[oa_url]], and
supplies a [[pdf_url]] only where the providers left none.  The call is cheap
when it cannot help---no DOI, no contact email, or an already-cached DOI---so
it is safe to make on every enrichment.

When even Unpaywall leaves [[pdf_url]] empty, the paper has exhausted
every lookup keyed on its own identity, and the preprint fallback
(\cref{preprint-module}) takes over: it may \emph{replace} the entry
with a preprint version of the work so the citation points at the
version the user can actually read.  This is the one enrichment step
that can change a paper's identity, which is why it runs last and only
when the PDF is still missing---an entry whose publisher PDF was found
must never be swapped.  The [[preprint_search]] flag is passed through
to [[find_preprint]]; the authoritative link tiers always run.

Providers are not perfectly consistent in DOI lookups: some omit the DOI field
in the returned record, and occasionally a lookup yields a non-matching work.
Because [[Paper]] identity uses the DOI when present, we treat a missing DOI in
a DOI lookup response as the requested DOI and skip merges that do not match.

<<enrichment functions>>=
# Default fields enriched via DOI lookup.
ENRICHABLE_FIELDS = ["title", "authors", "abstract", "year", "venue", "pdf_url"]


def enrich_paper(
    paper: Paper,
    fields: list[str] | None = None,
    providers: list[str] | None = None,
    fetch_refs_cites: bool = False,
    preprint_search: bool = False,
    outcome: LookupOutcome | None = None,
    judge_abstract: bool = False,
) -> Paper:
    """
    Enrich a paper by fetching missing fields from other providers.

    This function fills in any missing metadata by looking up the paper's DOI
    in providers with better coverage. It's useful for:
    - Papers from DBLP/WOS that lack abstracts
    - Papers from snowballing that may only have a DOI
    - Any paper missing title, authors, abstract, year, venue, or pdf_url

    When [[fetch_refs_cites]] is True, also fetches references and citations
    if they are not already present on the paper. The refs/cites are stored
    directly on the Paper object, so they persist with any caching.

    Args:
        paper: The paper to enrich.
        fields: Fields to check and enrich. If None, checks all enrichable
            fields (title, authors, abstract, year, venue, pdf_url).
        providers: Provider names to try, in priority order. If None, uses
            [[ENRICHMENT_PROVIDERS]].
        fetch_refs_cites: If True, also fetch references and citations.
        preprint_search: If True, the preprint fallback may also search
            preprint servers by title and authors; authoritative
            DOI-based preprint links are followed regardless.
        outcome: If given, filled with the providers asked, the lookups
            that failed and why, and a failed Unpaywall lookup; also with
            the source of an abstract this call stored and a doubtful
            abstract it met.
        judge_abstract: If True, an abstract `doubtful_abstract` flags
            does not count as one: further sources are asked, and the
            first abstract from them that is not doubtful replaces it.
            Leave False for an abstract given by hand.

    Returns:
        A new Paper with enriched fields, or the original if enrichment
        fails or isn't needed. When only a preprint of the paper has a
        readable PDF, the returned entry is that preprint (a different
        DOI), with the published DOI in `published_versions`.
    """
    if fields is None:
        fields = ENRICHABLE_FIELDS

    result = paper
    plain_abstract = None  # an abstract service's text, already plain
    judging = judge_abstract and "abstract" in fields

    # Derive canonical arXiv DOI from arXiv URLs.
    # This stabilizes paper identity and enables DOI-based enrichment for
    # arXiv papers that were returned without a DOI.
    if not result.doi:
        derived = derive_arxiv_doi(result)
        if derived:
            result = replace(result, doi=derived)

    <<note an abstract held doubtful>>
    # Check if metadata enrichment is needed
    if needs_enrichment(result, fields, judging):
        # Need DOI for lookup
        if not result.doi:
            logger.debug(
                f"Paper '{result.title_preview()}' has no DOI, cannot enrich"
            )
        else:
            logger.debug(
                f"Enriching paper '{result.title_preview()}' "
                f"(DOI: {result.doi})"
            )

            providers_to_try = providers or ENRICHMENT_PROVIDERS

            # Try each enrichment provider in order
            for provider_name in providers_to_try:
                if not needs_enrichment(result, fields, judging):
                    break
                <<skip an abstract service with nothing to fill>>

                provider = enrichment_source(provider_name)
                if provider is None:
                    continue

                if hasattr(provider, "is_available") and not provider.is_available():
                    continue

                if not hasattr(provider, "get_paper_by_doi"):
                    continue

                logger.debug(f"Trying to enrich from {provider_name}")
                doi = result.doi
                if not doi:
                    # Defensive: this branch should be unreachable because we
                    # only enter enrichment when the paper has a DOI.
                    continue

                enriched = cast(Any, provider).get_paper_by_doi(doi)
                <<note the outcome of the provider's lookup>>
                if enriched is None:
                    logger.debug(
                        f"{provider_name} returned no data for DOI {result.doi}"
                    )
                    continue

                # Some providers return partial records without a DOI even when
                # queried by DOI. For enrichment merges we treat the provider
                # record as representing the requested DOI.

                if not enriched.doi:
                    enriched = replace(enriched, doi=doi)

                # Merge the enriched data into our paper.
                # We want to keep our paper's existing data and fill in gaps.
                <<let a real abstract replace a doubtful one>>
                try:
                    result = base.merge_with(enriched)
                    <<note an abstract a service supplied>>
                    <<note where the stored abstract came from>>
                    logger.info(
                        f"Successfully enriched paper from {provider_name}"
                    )
                except ValueError:
                    # Provider returned a record that does not match the paper
                    # identity implied by the DOI lookup; skip it and keep
                    # trying other providers.
                    logger.debug(
                        "Skipping enrichment merge from %s for DOI %s: "
                        "non-matching record",
                        provider_name,
                        doi,
                    )
                    continue
    else:
        logger.debug(f"Paper '{paper.title_preview()}' doesn't need metadata enrichment")

    # Open-access status and a fallback PDF link from Unpaywall
    if "pdf_url" in fields and result.doi:
        result = unpaywall.annotate_paper(result)
        <<note the outcome of the open-access lookup>>

    # Still no PDF: try to adopt a preprint version instead.
    if "pdf_url" in fields and not result.pdf_url:
        replacement = preprint.find_preprint(
            result, search=preprint_search
        )
        if replacement is not None:
            <<forget the source of an abstract the adoption replaced>>
            result = replacement

    # Fetch references and citations if requested
    if fetch_refs_cites and result.doi:
        result = _fetch_refs_cites(result)

    # Fetched text carries provider markup like search results do.
    return clean_paper_text_except(result, plain_abstract)


@

Fetched text carries the markup of the provider's system, as search
results do, and the whole paper is cleaned by [[clean_paper_text]]
(\cref{sec:clean-provider-text}) before it is returned.
That also repairs what the paper held: entities and mojibake in the
title, venue or abstract of a session recorded before the cleaning
existed are cleaned in the session itself by the next [[scholar
enrich]].

One text is left alone: an abstract Crossref or Europe PMC supplied in
this very call.
It is plain text already ([[markup_to_text]],
\cref{sec:markup-to-text}), which has decoded the entities, and
[[clean_metadata_text]] is not idempotent: it decodes entities again
and then removes the inline tags it knows, so an abstract that says
[[<i>]]---read from a JATS [[&lt;i&gt;]]---would lose those characters.
The exception is that narrow on purpose: everything else is cleaned as
it always was.

