\subsection{Replacing a doubtful abstract}
\label{sec:replace-doubtful-abstract}

An abstract that [[doubtful_abstract]] flags (\cref{sec:doubtful-abstracts})
is the service's text and may be all there is, so it is never dropped.
But it should not end the search for a real one either.
When the caller asks for judging ([[judge_abstract]]), enrichment
therefore treats it as follows:
\begin{description}
\item[It does not satisfy the field] [[needs_enrichment]] counts it as
  missing, so the remaining sources are asked, the abstract services
  included.
\item[A real abstract replaces it] the first abstract from a later
  source that is \emph{not} doubtful takes its place, whether the
  doubtful text was held before the call or supplied earlier in it.
\item[Otherwise it stays] when no source has a better one, the doubtful
  text is kept: a later doubtful text does not replace an earlier one,
  since neither is better.
\end{description}
An abstract that is not doubtful is never replaced, as before: the
merge keeps the stored value.
Only a doubtful one is set aside, and only for a text that is not.

Two costs come with this.
A doubtful abstract that no source can better is looked up again on
every run, at one lookup per source asked, since nothing records that
the sources were asked already; the Web of Science and Scopus DOI
lookups are not cached, so they are asked anew each time.
A paper without an abstract costs the same today, for the same reason,
so no cache of \enquote{asked, nothing better} is added here.
And a DOI lookup can return the wrong record: an answer for another
work that still passes the merge's identity check (a record without a
DOI is taken for the DOI asked, above) may then replace a doubtful
abstract with that work's text.
The replaced text is not lost: the command keeps it in the decision's
record of the fetched abstract, with its reason and the source of the
new text ([[replaced]], \cref{sec:fetched-abstract} in the review
module), so it can be recovered and the source named.

The replacement is done by merging into a copy of the paper without
its abstract, so that [[Paper.merge_with]] fills it from the source as
it fills any gap.
If the source's record does not match the paper, the merge raises and
the paper is kept as it was, doubtful abstract included.

<<let a real abstract replace a doubtful one>>=
base = result
if (judging and enriched.abstract
        and doubtful_abstract(result.abstract) is not None
        and doubtful_abstract(enriched.abstract) is None):
    base = replace(result, abstract=None)
@

Judging is off by default, and [[scholar enrich]] turns it on per paper
(\cref{sec:enrich-doubtful-abstracts} in the CLI module).
Only the caller knows whether an abstract was given by hand
([[sessions set-abstract]], recorded on the review decision, not on the
paper), and a hand-given abstract is never judged or replaced: the
screener read it and chose it.
The TUI and [[search --enrich]] do not judge, so they enrich as they
always did.

\paragraph{Where the abstract came from.}
[[Paper.sources]] names the providers of a paper, not of each field,
so it cannot say which service supplied the abstract.
The outcome of the call ([[LookupOutcome]],
\cref{sec:enrich-lookup-outcomes}) therefore notes it: the source whose
abstract the call stored, in [[abstract_from]], and the first doubtful
abstract the call met, held or supplied, with its reason and source.
The command stores the source with the decision
(\cref{sec:enrich-doubtful-abstracts} in the CLI module), and the tally
counts the doubtful ones by what became of them.
A source supplied the abstract when the paper had none to merge into
([[base]] has none) and has one after the merge.

